He does not rise as I enter, but the light gutters out. The sigils on his veil darken.

"I found your name." My voice sounds small in the vaulted dark. "In the deep archive. A ritual script. Instructions for the binding—and for its failure."

The wrappings shift. Not surprise. Something closer to recognition. "You should not have been able to read that script. It was sealed."

"The seal is failing. Like everything else." I cross the room, holding the vellum with his name on it. "You knew. You've always known the binding was designed to break. That's why you needed a cartographer. Not to map the court—to map the failure. To find the six seals before they shatter."

He is silent for a long time. The ward-chamber hums around us, the heartstone's pulse a slow, steady count.

"Yes." The word is barely a whisper of cloth on cloth. "I have been waiting for someone who could read what I cannot. Someone who could see the lies in the stone and not look away."

I press my thumb behind my left eye. The frost is quiet. He is telling the truth.

"How long do we have?"

"Months. Perhaps less. The seals degrade faster each year. When the first one fails, the others will follow. The binding will shatter. The court will unmake itself." He turns, and even through the wrappings I can feel the weight of two centuries pressing down on him. "And I will be unmade with it."

"Then we don't let the first seal fail." I hold up the vellum. "Show me where they are. All six. We map them. We reinforce them. We buy time."

"Time for what?"

I meet the place where his eyes should be. "Time for me to find your name. The real one. The one that was bound into the heartstone and shattered. If I can reconstruct it—if I can speak it—the binding won't just hold. It will be complete. The court will be anchored. Not by a cage, but by a choice."

The ward-chamber is silent. The heartstone pulses.

"You would risk your life for a court that has given you nothing but pain and suspicion?"

I think of Mira's hands, flour-dusted, rolling dough. Of Rhys's pale eyes, sharp as obsidian. Of Thessa's cold fingers and her ghost of a smile. Of the garden where night-blooms pulse with a light that has no name.

"I would risk my life for the people who have become mine. And for the man who left a flower on my pillow and watched me from the shadows because he couldn't bear to be seen."

The wrappings shift. Something that might be a breath. Something that might be the beginning of hope.

"Then we begin," he says."You could refuse," Mira says again, and the frost behind my eye doesn't move. She's telling the truth — she believes I could. But belief and permission are different countries, and I've spent my life mapping the border between them.

"I could," I say. "But I won't."

I don't explain about the charter, about my mother's health, about the obligations my father has spent years drawing around me like contour lines on a map. Mira knows. She's been there for every one of those lines — the night my mother's cough first brought blood to her handkerchief, the morning my father sat at his desk and didn't move for six hours, the afternoon I mapped my first lie and realized the world was not what it told me it was.

She nods once. Then: "Then I'm coming with you."

"Mira—"

"I'm coming. Don't argue. You'll lose." She pushes off the doorframe and crouches beside me, dark hair falling forward. "Someone needs to keep you alive in whatever that court is. And I've always wanted to see the Mire."

"Fine. But you ride your own horse."

"Obviously."

She leaves. I'm alone with my instruments and the low, persistent ache behind my left eye. I press my thumb to it and think about the Ashmark. About a man without a name. About what it would feel like to stand in a room with someone and not feel the frost.

Then I stop thinking about it, because the territory I'm heading into is unmapped, and I need my instruments more than I need my imagination.

I finish packing. The journals go into the case. The compass goes into my pocket. The lie-sense — the gift, the curse, the thing I've never asked for and can't put down — goes with me, as it always does, a frost-needle waiting behind my left eye for the next lie to measure.

Tomorrow, I ride for the Withered Court.

Tonight, I press my thumb behind my eye and feel the ache that has been my companion for fifteen years, and I think: *What if the map is wrong? What if the territory I'm walking into has no lies to measure?*

The compass doesn't answer. Compasses never do.

But for the first time in my life, the silence feels like permission.

